# ~/.hellishrc — sourced by interactive hellish sessions (the .bashrc analogue).
#
# AI is ON by default in interactive mode. Turn it off any time with:
#     set +o ai
#
# ── AI backend ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Out of the box hellish talks to a LOCAL llama.cpp server at 127.0.0.1:8080.
# Start one (a model lives in a docker volume; pulled once):
#     make ai-pull MODEL=<https url to a .gguf>
#     make ai-up                 # CPU (universal). Or: COMPUTE=hybrid / COMPUTE=gpu
# See docs/AI-COMPUTE.md for the CPU / hybrid / GPU trade-offs.
#
# For crazy-fast + high-intelligence answers, point it at a cloud API instead.
# Easiest — run once and it writes the block below for you:
#     ai setup groq            # free key from console.groq.com (prompted, hidden)
#     ai setup openrouter      # Claude / many models via OpenRouter
#     ai setup openai          # gpt-4o-mini, etc.
#
# …or configure by hand (any OpenAI-compatible API). A cloud primary falls back
# to the local server automatically on error / rate-limit:
#     export HELLISH_AI_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions
#     export HELLISH_AI_KEY=gsk_your_key_here
#     export HELLISH_AI_MODEL=llama-3.3-70b-versatile
#
# Full reference: docs/AI.md   (commands, keybindings, env vars, privacy).
